    "created_at": "2021-09-20T16:05:57Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-09-20T16:05:57Z",
    "body": "Personally I like the distinction Jeffrey is proposing here, with \"implementation\" corresponding to something more like \"Firefox\", and \"user agent\" being the concrete thing that we hang associations off of in specs. (And thus mapping to individual browser profiles or modes.)\r\n\r\nI think this fits well with the intuition behind \"implementation-defined\" as well, if we tweak the wording in the way I suggested. That is, given this division, both \"up to the user agent\" and \"up to the implementation\" allow the same amount of freedom in theory, but \"up to the implementation\" suggests that in practice most such decisions are made at a per-browser level, not a per-profile level, which I think is generally true. (E.g., for \"lazy load root margin\", probably Firefox has a single global algorithm for determining lazy load root margin; it doesn't store a different value per-profile. Similarly for the implementation-defined string that means \"Submit\" for `<input type=submit>`.)\r\n\r\nI also don't think too many downstream specs would need updating. I think most downstream specs use either \"user agent\" as the thing to hang associations off of, or \"implementation-defined\". Both would work well with what's proposed here. Of course, a full audit would be ideal, but I believe our tooling still doesn't allow that.",
